==English==

===Etymology===
From {{etyl|frm|en}} {{term|convenir|lang=frm}}, from {{etyl|la|en}} ''[[convenire]]; [[con-]] + [[venire]]'' (to come).

===Pronunciation===
* {{IPA|/ˈkɒn.vɪin/|/kənˈvɪin/}} {{X-SAMPA|/"kQn.vi:n/|/k@n"vi:n/}} {{a|UK}}

===Verb===
{{en-verb|conven|ing}}

# {{context|intransitive|lang=en}} To come together; to [[meet]]; to [[unite]].
#* Isaac Newton
#*: In short-sighted men {{...}} the rays converge and '''convene''' in the eyes before they come at the bottom.
# {{context|intransitive|lang=en}} To come together, as in one body or for a public purpose; to meet; to [[assemble]].
#* Sir R. Baker
#*: The Parliament of Scotland now '''convened'''.
#* Thomson
#*: Faint, underneath, the household fowls '''convene'''.
#{{senseid|en|to cause to assemble, to call together}}{{context|transitive|lang=en}} To cause to assemble; to call together; to [[convoke]].
# {{context|transitive|lang=en}} To summon [[judicially]] to meet or appear.

====Synonyms====
* to [[meet]]
* to [[assemble]]
* to [[congregate]]
* to [[collect]]
* to [[unite]]
* to [[summon]]
* to [[convoke]]

====Derived terms====
* [[convention]]
* [[convener]], [[convenor]]

